WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:07.330
So it is today the 18th anniversary of nine
11. It's incredible. It has actually been,

00:00:07.330 --> 00:00:14.950
uh, more than half of my life has been lived
after nine 11 than before nine 11, which is

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:19.780
incredible. It's also incredible just how
fast time passes and with each passing year

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:28.000
since nine 11, uh, since the program has existed,
uh, I have been increasingly unsure, I guess

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:33.300
I would say, or kind of just ambivalent about
what should we be doing? Should we just be

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:38.739
doing the same thing every nine 11 anniversary
more or less? I don't know. It's actually

00:00:38.739 --> 00:00:42.230
been a few years since we've even talked about
it because for the last several anniversaries,

00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:47.100
I just didn't really have anything new to
add. So what I want to think about today is

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:52.190
I will retell the story of, of where I was
on nine 11, which I remember vividly, although

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:57.359
behavioral economists and others would say,
my memory of this has deteriorated over the

00:00:57.359 --> 00:01:01.719
years and it is no longer accurate, but you
can go back and check contrast with the first

00:01:01.719 --> 00:01:02.909
time I told the story.

00:01:02.909 --> 00:01:07.620
And then I also want to talk about the, the
sort of legacy of nine 11 as I now see it.

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:14.720
So, uh, on, on the morning of nine 11, I was
in high school and I was sitting in psychology

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:20.640
class with a teacher of mine who I really
liked Donald Palmisano, who sadly is no longer

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:27.350
with us. He actually passed away not that
long ago and I got the chance to see him out

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:33.760
in western Massachusetts at an event a few
years ago. And, um, I remember his wife actually

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:40.230
came up to me having heard my description
of, uh, my nine 11 morning and said, you know

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:43.730
what, when I heard you tell that story, I
asked him, is it, do you think it really went

00:01:43.730 --> 00:01:47.390
down that way? And he said, you know, I don't
remember, but it sounds sort of like how it

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:52.030
would go down. So I was sitting in psychology
class and, um, a student who was sort of a

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:55.650
regular troublemaker, whose name I forgot,
I think his name might have been Eric.

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:59.659
And it's amazing that his, the, the student's
name, I forgotten. He was always out of his

00:01:59.659 --> 00:02:04.250
seat and always walking around in the halls.
He had gone to the bathroom and he came back

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:11.220
into the room, uh, agitated and interrupted
psychology class and said to Mr Palmisano,

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:16.150
we're under attack. It's happening in New
York. Things are crazy. More or less just

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:20.810
saying we were, we're under attack. The country's
under attack. And Mr. Paul Massano looked

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:26.379
at him in the context of this is a guy who's
always interrupting class and sort of a troublemaker

00:02:26.379 --> 00:02:30.219
and not particularly well behaved and just
didn't believe it and said, you know what,

00:02:30.219 --> 00:02:35.249
I'm sure everything's fine. Please just sit
down. And of course we later found out that

00:02:35.249 --> 00:02:40.000
it was true. And, um, I don't think that there
was early dismissal, but I certainly went

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:41.939
home and was just watching TV.

00:02:41.939 --> 00:02:47.230
Uh, the rest of the day. And the following
day Mr Palmisano actually apologized to the

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:53.319
student and said, you know what? You were
right. I apologize for how I reacted. And,

00:02:53.319 --> 00:02:57.900
uh, it's, it's horrible what's happened. And
we just had a conversation in psychology class

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:05.919
about what had gone on. Um, since then with
each passing year. Uh, in some senses our

00:03:05.919 --> 00:03:11.230
perspective on what took place changes and
evolves partially just because when things

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:17.019
are less recent, our point of view is altered.
I don't think any of us can control this also,

00:03:17.019 --> 00:03:23.879
partially because we know more about what
the legacy has been. And for me, unfortunately

00:03:23.879 --> 00:03:30.269
the legacy of nine 11 in the United States
has been pretty bad. Uh, we started a war

00:03:30.269 --> 00:03:35.549
in the wrong place as a result of nine 11
against people who didn't do the thing that

00:03:35.549 --> 00:03:38.430
we claimed was the reason for starting the
war.

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:45.659
We have seen pervasive, persistent and also
effective privacy invasions as a result of

00:03:45.659 --> 00:03:52.319
nine 11 under the guise of national security.
We've seen the Patriot Act, we've seen NSA

00:03:52.319 --> 00:03:57.121
surveillance, all of that stuff that we've
talked about over the last many years. Post

00:03:57.121 --> 00:04:02.469
nine 11 that the David Pakman show has been
going on, we have become really accustomed

00:04:02.469 --> 00:04:11.590
to the persistent state of the global war
on terror. Uh, the permanent war status quo

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:17.280
of we're always somewhere for many years it's
been Afghanistan, a presence in Iraq that

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:24.230
never seems to end. Other potential engagements,
all sort of under this post nine 11 mentality

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:29.140
of we've got to make sure that people out
there who want to harm us aren't able to do

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:33.800
it and that we're projecting strength and
all of this stuff. One of the elements of

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:39.660
the legacy of nine 11 sort of the, the high
points, not in terms of them being morally

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:45.790
good or bad, but just notable was the Obama
administration capture of Osama bin Laden.

00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:49.400
And then of course subsequently everything
we learned about the lead up to that and the

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:54.970
people that were involved. We've seen a legacy
of continued ignorance and it's really more

00:04:54.970 --> 00:04:59.390
than ignorance. It's really more of a deliberate
kind of bearing our heads in the sand, many

00:04:59.390 --> 00:05:07.070
of us about what creates radicalism through
foreign policy. And this is not, you know,

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:12.900
very often when we talk about foreign policy
creating radicals who want to attack the United

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:20.340
States, it becomes a very sort of mannequin
white, black thing of listen, either people

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:26.660
are responsible for choosing to attack the
United States, or you're saying that they

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:31.610
lack all responsibility and it's just the
fault of the u s through foreign policy for

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:36.840
radicalizing people. And of course the answer
is no, that's not the black and white scenario

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:41.320
that anyone who's thoughtful is trying to
lay out. One of the things we've hopefully

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:48.340
learned over the 18 years since nine 11, is
that radicalization, both domestically and

00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:55.050
geopolitically, whether we're talking about
white supremacy or or Islamic, uh, um, extremism

00:05:55.050 --> 00:06:02.550
or whatever the case may be, is a combination
of, uh, circumstances domestically, which

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:09.130
are influenced by the foreign policies of
super powers like the United States combined

00:06:09.130 --> 00:06:19.040
with the particular, uh, predispositions and,
uh, sort of, uh, circumstances of individuals.

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:23.040
All of the white supremacists and former white
supremacists. I've interviewed on the show

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:32.180
have told a very consistent story about how
they know who is sort of ripe for radicalization

00:06:32.180 --> 00:06:39.270
and indoctrination and in the same way when
we talk about al Qaeda or isis or whatever,

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:45.190
we're not excusing the actions of individuals
when we say we should consider the role that

00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:52.060
the relationships between countries and American
foreign policy have on creating an environment

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:58.170
ripe for radicalization. While understanding
that millions, tens of millions, hundreds

00:06:58.170 --> 00:07:04.380
of millions, billions of people on the planet
are not apt to be radicalized regardless of

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:08.770
foreign policy. These things are not black
and white and hopefully if there's a positive

00:07:08.770 --> 00:07:12.080
legacy from nine 11 and I know that for a
lot of people this just doesn't work, but

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:16.690
hopefully the more thinking people are aware
of this stuff, hopefully one of the positive

00:07:16.690 --> 00:07:19.780
legacies of nine 11 if there's anything to
find in it, is that we have sort of deepened

00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:25.380
our understanding of the interaction between
personal circumstances, personal responsibility

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:30.540
and geopolitical status quo in creating radicalization
around the world.

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:36.460
We also have seen continued neo-conservative
war hawking on a lot of the right and even

00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:42.250
on some of the left as a result of nine 11.
Um, in terms of even justifying, uh, attempted

00:07:42.250 --> 00:07:47.200
or would be engagements in other parts of
the world over the last 18 years. Airport

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:53.630
security and the establishment of the TSA
of course a major legacy of nine 11 and unfortunately

00:07:53.630 --> 00:08:01.910
not one of the best ones. TSA, a failing to
identify banned items in almost 95% of internal

00:08:01.910 --> 00:08:09.930
self tests, drills, so to speak of their security
processes. So sadly it is mostly negative.

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:14.780
There was, I mean, if we want to find something
that was sort of good that happened after

00:08:14.780 --> 00:08:20.930
nine 11, um, that of course doesn't outweigh
any of the bed. There was actually a short

00:08:20.930 --> 00:08:27.650
lived positive sort of sense of unity in the
United States where hyper partisanship was

00:08:27.650 --> 00:08:30.080
mitigated a little bit.

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:35.779
And it was about coming together as a country
and helping people in New York City and in

00:08:35.779 --> 00:08:43.240
Washington, D C and all of the people affected
by nine 11. And it didn't last long. And it's

00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:50.160
not surprising that it was these sort of black
swan type events. To take a term from, uh,

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:56.270
Nassim Taleb who has written about such events,
um, that really kind of shook up the status

00:08:56.270 --> 00:09:01.570
quo. It didn't last very long. And of course
then the administration of George W. Bush

00:09:01.570 --> 00:09:06.610
choosing to retaliate to nine 11 by going
into Iraq, which had nothing to do with it,

00:09:06.610 --> 00:09:14.080
did not help at all. My question to you, how
should we be remembering nine 11 as we get

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:19.700
closer and closer to 20 years out from the
event, which is just incredible that almost

00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:26.340
20 years have gone by. Uh, and what do you
see as the legacy of nine 11 on geopolitics

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:30.940
on United States foreign policy, domestic,
uh, issues and the status quo? I want to hear

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:31.370
from you.

